      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-\\frac{8}{3}$ and $y=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-\\frac{8}{3},0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0).$",
